@@ -3413,3 +3413,17 @@ cheaper and more honest and was nearly built first; ticks won only because it is
 **KERNEL-69.2 (2026-10-26, TRUST-SEED; cv 01a179ea-85e3): every World an honest fold reaches carries the genesis authority.** `Kernel/Proofs/AuthorityOf.lean`: `derive_born_not_authority` (no receiver bears a `kindAuthority` cell anywhere in a call's tree: `enroll` a member, `install` a package, `create` an object, `grant` a grant, `issue`/`account` an account, the rest nothing; one case per `Body` arm, `derive_all`) and `receive_born_not_authority`; `AuthorityOnly X auth w` (every live `kindAuthority` cell is `authorityCell auth` at `authorityId`, every nullifier slot holds `spent`), kept by `admit` of a Turn bearing no such cell (a write of the authority cell is refused: ROM) and by every recorded refusal, so by every honest log (`honest_authorityOnly`); `authorityOf_of_only` (a canonical World where that holds and the cell is live answers `auth`); and the consumers' form, `authorityOf_honest_fold`: from a checked genesis, along any `Honest` log (the writer's and every follower's World), `authorityOf w' = some auth`. `authorityOf_genesisWorld` is now its zero-step instance through `authorityOf_of_only` (statement unchanged; its trie walk is not repeated). Hypotheses added to landed theorems: 0.
 
 **Floor.** A receiver that bears a `kindAuthority` cell (say `issue` writing kind 5): one honest Turn leaves two live cells of the kind, `authorityOf` answers `none`, and every certified-head consumer refuses a World it should accept. `derive_born_not_authority` fails on that arm by name. Not covered, named: a DISHONEST log (a Turn no receiver derives) may bear such a cell; `admit` does not refuse it, and the consumers that need the authority of a World they did not re-derive read it at genesis or adoption, where `atGenesis` and the invitation pin already authenticate it.
+
+## KERNEL-70 · Retirement: an object or an account ends by its owner's signed `retire`, judged by its laws; a grant by its issuer; its storage comes back and its id stays a tombstone (2026-10-26; RETIRE, RETIRE-2; Ember's decision cv 01a16d3e-5358; with LANG's `Occasion.retire` arms; CROSS-LAYER: LANG, LEDGER, HOSTING, PROTOCOL, CLIENT, ROOT/SPEC)
+
+**Problem.** Nothing but a grant could end. An object, a data cell or an account, once born, held its storage forever: the kernel already credited a retirement back to the allowance (KERNEL-19), already dropped a retired parent's relation heads (KERNEL-63) and already refused a parent still holding rows (`rowsRemain`), but no receiver derived a retirement of anything but a grant (`Body.revoke`). So the World's allowance only fell, and every theorem that said "an object live at a log's start is live at its end" (KERNEL-28's totality, KERNEL-64's head, Spec's B3) was true for want of a receiver, not by design.
+
+**Decision.** ONE act, `Body.retire cell`, replaces `Body.revoke` (codec tag 8, bytes unchanged; the verb `revoke` is `retire CELL`). `retireCheck` decides by the pinned live cell's kind: a GRANT by its issuer (`notIssuer`: the old revocation, every chain through it dying with it); an OBJECT (data or programmed) by its owner (`notOwner`), and every law in force — the package's law arm, then the cell's extra laws — judges the new occasion `Occasion.retire` over its last state (old = new), so a law that refuses `retire` makes its object permanent; an ACCOUNT by its owner (`notOwner`), only once balance and floor are both 0 (`valueRemains`), so a retirement moves no value and a well that bounds a supply is never retired; a MEMBER, a PACKAGE or the AUTHORITY: `notRetirable`. The one effect is `(cell, .retire)`; `World.admit` credits the cell's weight and its heads' (KERNEL-19, KERNEL-63) and leaves a tombstone, terminal: nothing is born at the id again (`idTaken`). `retire` is not callable from a method (cv 01a17ad8-5dcf: an object-owned account or an object-issued grant needs a `retire` call arm in Lang's call type). Refusals `notOwner`, `valueRemains`, `notRetirable` join the receive table after `notIssuer` (every later code shifts).
+
+**Theorems.** `retire_next` (a derived retire of a live object: its owner slot names the signer, and the laws in force judged `retire` from its last state to the same state); `retire_next_tomb` (admitted, it leaves the tombstone); `retire_final` (every World any later fold reaches holds the tombstone and no live cell at the id); `retire_frees_credit` (the allowance gains the object's entry weight once, plus what its heads give back, less the charge; kernel-sim `retire-object:ok` measures the same identity); tooth `effect_at_object_needs_owner` (replaces `effect_at_object_needs_kind`: without its owner check, the receiver derives a stranger's retirement). Restated with a retire case, each said: KERNEL-28's totality (`effect_at_object` gains the disjunct "the signed retire of it, act retire"; `object_turn_cases` gains `body = .retire obj`, the keep case `body ≠ .retire obj`; `dispatch_other`/`dispatched_effects` exclude retire); `ObjectStep` gains `retire` (JUDGED: the package and the cell's laws, request `reqOf … .retire`, old = new, calls []) and `dead` (any turn at a World holding the object's tombstone records nothing); `object_step_some` WEAKENS where it must — its run conjunct and its "live after" conjunct hold outside a retirement, and a retirement ends at the tombstone; `object_history_runs` WEAKENS the same way (a retirement judged by a package ran no method; Ledger's `escrow_invokes_one_payout` is unchanged: a retirement returns no calls, and the escrow's law refuses `retire` anyway); `object_history_of_honest` gains the retire step followed by `object_history_of_dead`; `object_history_chain` (THE LIFT) is UNCHANGED in statement: a retire step is a `Std.Step` like a relaw (old = new) and nothing follows it (`object_history_dead`). KERNEL-64: `turn_object_head`, `submit_object_head`, `step_object_head`, `honest_object_head`, `fold_package_fixed` gain the disjunct "or a tombstone" (the package is fixed WHILE THE OBJECT LIVES); Protocol `absorbStep_object_head` and `PackageAt` the same; Hosting's `invokedPackage_at` and `answered_row_committed` keep their statements (a retired object derives no invoke). Spec `Integrity.law` (B3): its package conjunct reads "live with the same package, or retired" — a change of ROOT's top-level claim because the object can now end, not a weakened check; `floor_integrity_law` still refutes the raw writer (its run ends with the object live under a moved package). Versions' `row_stale_behind_forever`/`range_stale_behind_forever` tombstone disjunct, `rowStep_dead`/`admit_dead` (`orphanRow`), `checkRowsRemain` and `dropHeads(U)`'s retire arm are now REACHABLE through receive (journey RT5–RT10).
+
+**Grants over a retired cell (the dangling-grant bias).** The bias to answer: a retire that leaves grants nobody can use or revoke. As built: a grant TARGETING a retired object is dead — `retire_final`: no admitted turn ever writes, invokes, relaws or re-creates the object again, whoever signs through whatever grant; a derivation that reads it finds no cell (`missingCell`), and a call pinned at its last live entry is `stale` (RT8). The grant cell itself stays live and its issuer can still `retire` it, taking its storage back (the grant's own retirement is the issuer's act, unchanged). MEMBERS are not retirable, by design: a member roots authority no cell lists (the owner of cells, the issuer of grants), so its end would leave grants unrevocable and objects ownerless — exactly the bias; its storage is bounded by enrolment counts (KERNEL-16); a member's departure needs an index of what it roots (cv 01a17ad8-5df4). A PROGRAMMED object's end is its package's law's decision, as every act of it is; the Std laws' `retire` arms are conservative (ownerSigns: the owner; quorum, Std.head, Escrow, the machine, `sealed`, Timelock: refused; bounded/windowed: admitted, the state unchanged).
+
+**Critique.** (1) One act for four kinds, decided by the cell's kind, not four acts: a retirement is one transition of the World (the effect `retire`), and the kernel already had one admission rule for it; four Bodies would be four receivers whose only difference is who decides. The price: `retireCheck` is a case on the kind, and the refusal names say which case refused. (2) Tombstones are terminal, so an account id `(owner, asset)` is retired for good: the owner never holds that asset at that id again. A reusable id would let any third party (anyone may open anyone's account) re-spend the storage the owner gave back; terminal makes the owner's retirement durable. (3) Theorems that said "live at the end" now say "live at the end or retired". A consumer that needed the object live (Hosting's socket) recovers it from its own premise (an admitted invoke reads the object live), so nothing downstream lost a conclusion it used; the two that WEAKEN (`object_step_some`'s run/live conjuncts, `object_history_runs`) are said above. (4) `retire` judged with old = new means a law cannot see that the object is ending except through the occasion; that is the point (the occasion is in the request), and `Std.LawKeeps` names it with relaw and adopt (LANG). (5) Not built: retiring from a method; members' departure; a member-scale sweep of dead grants (a grant on a tombstone is cheap and its issuer's to retire).
+
+**What re-emits.** Every Store re-geneses (none deployed): `Occasion` gains `retire`, so every package's request type and every shipped package text re-id (world/doc, world/escrow, world/grain, world/head, world/hosting, world/resident, client fixtures foreign/pin); the receive refusal codes shift; codec vectors re-drawn (kernel/body, kernel/call, kernel/unsigned); `client/src/generated.rs` (STDIO_VERBS: `retire` for `revoke`); THE BAR's stale-base plant re-pinned. An old package text is refused (its request type is not the kernel's); a `revoke` verb is unknown.
